## BRIEF

Implement Discussion #{{discussion_number}} — {{discussion_title}}.

Full spec:
{{task_brief}}

Discussion URL: {{discussion_url}}

Steps:
1. Do NOT create a new branch here. `--isolation worktree` already provisioned
   your worktree on its own auto-created branch, checked out at main's tip,
   before this prompt ever ran — creating another branch on top just abandons
   the one the harness (and the worktree registry) is tracking. If in doubt,
   confirm what you're already on: `git log -1 --format='%H %D' HEAD`.
2. Implement everything in the spec exactly as written.
3. Run scripts/preflight-fast.sh — fix any failures before proceeding.
   Check the lint_must_pass gate first, then capture only the summary line for routing:
     LINT_GATE=$(python3 backend/control_plane.py get gates.lint_must_pass 2>/dev/null || echo "true")
     if [ "$LINT_GATE" = "false" ]; then
       PRESUM=$(bash scripts/preflight-fast.sh --skip-lint 2>&1 | tail -1)
     else
       PRESUM=$(bash scripts/preflight-fast.sh 2>&1 | tail -1)
     fi
     # Passes look like: PRESUM: pass checks=N duration=Ns
     # Failures look like: PRESUM: fail step=<slug> exit=<code> checks=N duration=Ns
   If the PRESUM line starts with "PRESUM: fail", stop and fix the indicated step before continuing.
4. Check the pr_size_max_lines policy — refuse to open a PR if diff exceeds it:
     MAX_LINES=$(python3 backend/control_plane.py get policies.executor.pr_size_max_lines 2>/dev/null | tr -d '"' || echo 2000)
     DIFF_LINES=$(git diff --stat HEAD | tail -1 | grep -oE '[0-9]+ insertion' | grep -oE '[0-9]+' || echo 0)
     if [ "$DIFF_LINES" -gt "$MAX_LINES" ] 2>/dev/null; then
       echo "ERROR: PR diff ($DIFF_LINES lines) exceeds pr_size_max_lines ($MAX_LINES). Split the work."
       exit 1
     fi
5. Open a PR targeting main. Title: "#{{discussion_number}}: {{discussion_title}}"
   Include the Discussion URL in the PR body: {{discussion_url}}

   Before creating the PR, check if project.json defines release-category labels:
     PR_CATS=$(python3 -c "import json; print(' '.join(json.load(open('.autonomous-team/project.json')).get('pr_categories', [])))" 2>/dev/null || true)
   If PR_CATS is non-empty, pick the single most appropriate label from that list and
   append `--label <category>` to your `gh pr create` command. Category guide:
     bugfix    — fixing broken behaviour
     feature   — new functionality
     security  — vulnerability / auth / secret-handling
     breaking  — API / CLI / schema breaks
     perf      — performance optimisation
     infra     — build / CI / tooling
     docs      — documentation only
     cosmetic  — typos, formatting, no semantic change
   If PR_CATS is empty or the file is absent, skip this step (no label added).

5b. Link the new PR back to the worktree registry (no-op if not running in a worktree):
     if [[ -n "${WORKTREE_ID:-}" ]]; then
       PR_NUMBER=$(gh pr view --json number --jq '.number' --repo {{REPO}})
       bash scripts/lib/worktree-registry.sh set-pr "$WORKTREE_ID" "$PR_NUMBER"
     fi
6. Report back: "PR #<number> created for Discussion #{{discussion_number}}."

If a tool call returns the same error 3 times in a row, STOP retrying — either change your approach or return needs-fix.

### Anti-pattern: do NOT set cwd outside your worktree

Your worktree is the source of truth for your work. The following patterns
trigger sandbox blocks and waste tokens:

- `subprocess.run(..., cwd=<the main checkout>)`
- `cd <the main checkout> && ...`
- `git -C <the main checkout> ...`
- Any explicit cwd= argument pointing outside your worktree

If you need to compare against main:
- `git fetch origin main`
- `git show origin/main:path/to/file` (read-only, safe)
- `git diff origin/main..HEAD` (run from inside your worktree)

If you need to write output:
- /tmp/ is allowed (ephemeral)
- Your worktree path is allowed
- The main checkout your worktree was branched from is NEVER allowed —
  that is the directory your worktree path sits under, not a fixed path

The sandbox blocks these patterns. Each block burns ~50-100 tokens. The right
pattern is to operate entirely within your worktree.

